### Eligible administrative costs

Administrative costs under 13 CFR 120.883 are **not Project costs**. Instead they are **added to the Net Debenture to arrive at the Gross Debenture**. That is why the Borrower contribution and the 40/35/30% shares are computed excluding them. (Fee details are in Section A, Ch. 4, Para. E.)

Examples of the Borrower's out-of-pocket costs that can be covered:
1. Settlement agent's fees;
2. Overnight delivery, postage and messenger services;
3. Certifications SBA requires (earthquake, flood, IRS, Certificate of Occupancy, certificate of completion);
4. Copying costs attributable to the above.

### Ineligible costs

**Any cost not directly attributable to or necessary for the Project** may not be paid with 504 proceeds (13 CFR 120.884). Examples from elsewhere in this chapter:
- Goodwill and receivables in a business acquisition;
- Attorney's fees for closing the Interim and Third Party Loans;
- Excess value of stock above the fixed assets it represents;
- Payments, distributions or loans to Associates of the Applicant (the credit memo must confirm none of the proceeds provide or refinance these).

### Ineligible 504 Projects (13 CFR 120.881)

1. **Relocation out of a community** - a Project cannot be approved if it relocates a business out of a community **and** will either cause a **net reduction of one-third of its jobs** or a **substantial increase in unemployment** in any area of the country. An exception may be allowed if the CDC justifies the relocation under 13 CFR 120.881(a)(1) and (a)(2).
2. **Projects in foreign countries.**

> **Watch out:** Relocation by itself is not disqualifying. The trigger is relocation out of a community combined with losing one-third of the jobs or causing substantial unemployment.

> **In practice:** Keep administrative costs on a separate line in any 504 sources-and-uses model. Mixing them into Project costs inflates the base used for the percentages and the job ratio.
